  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Consider the expansion of $(1+x)^n$. Show that\n$$\\binom{n}{0}+\\binom{n}{1}3+\\binom{n}{2}3^2+\\binom{n}{3}3^3+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}3^n=4^n.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{0}+\\binom{n}{1}3+\\binom{n}{2}3^2+\\binom{n}{3}3^3+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}3^n=4^n$.\n\nExpand $(1+x)^n$ and substitute $x=3$.",
      "worked": "By the binomial theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}(1+x)^n&=\\binom{n}{0}+\\binom{n}{1}x+\\binom{n}{2}x^2+\\binom{n}{3}x^3+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}x^n.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting $x=3$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{n}{0}+\\binom{n}{1}3+\\binom{n}{2}3^2+\\binom{n}{3}3^3+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}3^n&=(1+3)^n\\\\\\binom{n}{0}+\\binom{n}{1}3+\\binom{n}{2}3^2+\\binom{n}{3}3^3+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}3^n&=4^n.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
